# fixedbugs, 2026-09-08 (defect 021, found by panel 120's compiler-engineer;
# the reproducer below is shorter than the seat's).
#
# SYMPTOM: six lines with an ordinary generic identity function produced
# "`join` takes `[str]` and `str`, found `[#0]`". `#0` is an index into the
# checker's own type tables and is not a type anyone can write, which §4.17
# forbids in a message a reader is meant to act on.
#
# CAUSE: `selfhost/check/render.hero` renders a type parameter from
# `generics`, the letter list of the function the message is written FROM, and
# `check/state.hero` passes the CALLER's letters — so a callee's type
# parameter has no name there. The module doc said this "only happens where a
# caller outside the checker has no function in hand", and that premise was
# false of any user program. The premise had a test pinning `#0`, which is how
# it stayed comfortable.
#
# FIX: the loud honest noun, "a type parameter". Threading the callee's letters
# is the fuller repair and `check/walk.hero` already does it for two other
# messages, but that file stands at 1697 of a DECIDED 1700, so the thread
# waits for a ceiling raise and the message says the true thing meanwhile.

function id<T>(x: T) -> T
    return x

function main()
    xs = [1, 2, 3]
    print(join(xs.map(id).map(id), ", "))  #~ bad_operand

# Corrected 2026-10-06, defect 402, lane b13-gen402, below the program so line
# 27 stays line 27: the two messages above were not this line's mistake. Each
# `id` is a generic function used as a value, handed to `map`, a generic
# callee, through whose parameter nothing is bound (panel 105's flat rule), so
# nothing says what its `T` is. That is now told at each `id`, `cannot_infer`,
# naming `T` as the callee writes it: the fuller repair the paragraph above
# waited for, at this site. What the base told, `join` given `[a type
# parameter]` and an `i64` where a type parameter was expected, was the shadow
# of the unbound letter reaching the line's other calls.

# Corrected 2026-10-10, defect 541 (panel 203 R1), below the program so line
# 27 stays line 27: each `id` waits while `map`'s receiver settles `A`, and
# takes the instance at `i64`, so the two maps are accepted; what is told is
# the line's own mistake, `join` given the `[i64]` they make, its type as
# written (`bad_operand`). Panel 105's flat rule, which the correction above
# cites, is reversed for every argument with no type of its own.
